Subject: [PATCH] swiftshader: raise the maximum point size to 1024

ALIASED_POINT_SIZE_RANGE ends at 1023 on SwiftShader and 1024 on the Windows
D3D11 machines measured, and ANGLE passes the Vulkan limit straight through, so
the odd number is visible to any page that asks.

"Every real GPU" was the original claim here and it is wrong. 1024 is a D3D11
number, not a hardware one. Measured on an M4 Max through ANGLE/Metal the range
ends at 511, so a profile claiming an Apple device wants 511 and one claiming a
Windows device wants 1024. Which of those is right is the profile's decision;
this patch only stops 1023 — a value no shipping configuration reports — being
the ceiling the driver imposes on either.

Same class as patch 0027 and found the same way — by measurement, after the
profile clamp had taken everything it could. It belongs here rather than in the
clamp because the clamp only ever serves the smaller of the two numbers, and in
this one case the truth is the smaller number: reporting 1024 while the
rasteriser refuses anything above 1023 would be falsified by drawing a single
point.

Checked before changing, because a bare 1023 is exactly the shape of a
ten-bit field and this fork has been bitten by fixed-point overflow before.
It is not one. Two places read the constant — the reported limit, and a float
clamp on the point sprite in Renderer.cpp — and neither packs it.

On the path where no profile serves the GPU this raised value reaches the page
verbatim, the same way patch 0027's limits do: no compiled anchor was captured
through a software rasteriser, so patch 0093 leaves the GPU cluster
host-inherited and the page reads 1024 where stock Chromium's SwiftShader
reports 1023 — resources/fingerprints/raw/stock-linux-20260907T150627Z.json.
Serving a claim removes it, because the point-size clamp takes the smaller of
claim and driver. The remedy is a swiftshader-backend anchor in the catalogue,
which is a data item and not a change here.

What this patch does not close: nothing about how the software rasteriser draws
the point, or how long it takes. Patch 0027's prose has the measured throughput
and pixel-digest gap between the software and hardware paths, and patch 0091 is
the coherence answer to it.

Ledger: webgl.point-size-range.
diff --git a/third_party/swiftshader/src/Vulkan/VkConfig.hpp b/third_party/swiftshader/src/Vulkan/VkConfig.hpp
index cc4e3b9..b96d88a 100644
--- a/third_party/swiftshader/src/Vulkan/VkConfig.hpp
+++ b/third_party/swiftshader/src/Vulkan/VkConfig.hpp
@@ -94,7 +94,18 @@ constexpr uint32_t MAX_COMPUTE_WORKGROUP_INVOCATIONS = 256;
 
 constexpr size_t MAX_INLINE_UNIFORM_BLOCK_SIZE = 256;
 
-constexpr float MAX_POINT_SIZE = 1023.0;
+// Apostate: 1024.0 upstream 1023.0. Real GPUs report an aliased point size
+// range ending at 1024 and ANGLE passes the Vulkan limit straight through, so
+// SwiftShader's 1023 is visible as ALIASED_POINT_SIZE_RANGE[1] and is a value
+// no hardware reports.
+//
+// Raised rather than clamped in the profile because the clamp only ever takes
+// the smaller number, and here the truth is the smaller number: reporting 1024
+// while the rasteriser refuses anything above 1023 is falsified by drawing one
+// point. Only two things read this — the reported limit, and a float clamp on
+// the point sprite in Renderer.cpp — so there is no packed field to overflow,
+// which is the hazard that makes this class of change worth checking first.
+constexpr float MAX_POINT_SIZE = 1024.0;
 
 constexpr int MAX_SAMPLER_ALLOCATION_COUNT = 4000;
 
